- op: discuss  # 17c5
  at: '2026-08-26T01:08:07Z'  # 17c5
  by: agent  # 17c5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 17c5
  clock: 5  # 17c5
  nonce: 17c5  # 17c5
  message: "DECISION RECORDED BY THE AGENT, not the owner -- reversible, and the reasoning is here so it can be overturned on its merits rather than re-derived.\n\nThe owner directive was: if the ADRs, spec, tracker and history are enough to work out what they would probably want, decide it. I think they are. Here is the chain.\n\nWHAT THE STANDING RULING ALREADY SAYS. LIVE.md section 3 records a BOUND decision: \"Taint flows via IN-PROGRAM COMPUTATION, not a resource selected by it.\" That is measurement 0001's tie-break promoted to a standing ruling. A configured path handed to os.Chmod SELECTS the resource the sink acts on; it is not computed into anything. So the semantic question this item raises is already answered in principle -- resource selection is not taint flow.\n\nWHY THE ITEM STILL HAD A LIVE QUESTION. 0005's commissioned adjudication moved caddy:untrusted-input-no-host-fs:2 to TRUE POSITIVE, which is what prompted this filing. Re-reading it, that adjudication settled UNADJUDICABLE vs adjudicable -- whether the LoadModule hop is followable from in-repo declarations -- and not TP vs FP on the tie-break. It landed on TP without the tie-break being argued. So the tension is between a ruling and an adjudication that did not engage it, rather than between two rulings.\n\nWHAT 0006 ADDS. At least 6 of 38 surviving true positives (16%) have a sink whose tainted argument is a path the value merely selects (jaeger#2, jaeger#6, ArkLib#2, ArkLib#3, ArkLib#4, kamaraflow#4). That is a FLOOR: bash redirect situations are excluded because the record does not say whether the tainted value was the redirect TARGET or the written BYTES. Independently, 0006's pass-4 refuters applied the tie-break to refute the analogous open-handle shape (jaeger#0, ArkLib#0, ArkLib#1) without being told to.\n\nTHE DECISION: option (c) -- REPORT, BUT CLASSIFY DISTINCTLY. Introduce a visible distinction between a tainted value COMPUTED INTO the sink's payload and one that SELECTS the resource the sink acts on, and let a consumer filter on it.\n\nRejected alternatives and why:\n  (a) keep reporting them indistinguishably -- contradicts the standing ruling, and for any config-driven server it inflates finding volume with flows that are correct-but-uninformative. This item was filed because that is the status quo.\n  (b) suppress them -- loses genuinely dangerous findings. An attacker-controlled path reaching mkdir/open/chmod IS path traversal. Silently dropping it trades one wrong answer for a worse one.\n\nWHY (c) IS WHAT THIS PROJECT DOES. Every comparable call has gone the same way: dataflow_coverage publishes dataflow_capable:false with named blockers rather than hiding the gap; analyzer_disclosure ships dated positive declarations; verify-claims emits eight caveat kinds; INV-javam discloses uncatalogued languages; INV-tabaf discloses module_completeness grants. AGENTS.md requires \"explicit gaps over implicit completeness\". The pattern is: never silently drop, never silently conflate, give the consumer the distinction.\n\nSCOPE NOT DECIDED HERE. The mechanism is open -- a new evidence field, a caveat kind, or a boundary-vocabulary split are all viable, and the axis-declaration rules (ADR-0024) govern whichever is chosen. Whether existing findings are re-labelled or only new ones carry the distinction is also open. This decision settles the DIRECTION only.\n\nStatus moved off needs_human_review to todo_hard on that basis."  # 17c5
- op: discuss  # 8824
  at: '2026-08-27T21:05:46Z'  # 8824
  by: agent  # 8824
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8824
  clock: 6  # 8824
  nonce: '8824'  # 8824
  message: "OWNER RULED 2026-08-27: **B — keep it, and add a second number.**\n\nThe owner was given three options and chose B verbatim. In the owner's framing\nas put to them:\n\n  A — Keep it. Correctness only. Config flows stay TPs. Band stays unevaluable.\n  B — Keep it, and add a second number. Still a TP for correctness, but tag\n      configured-action flows structurally (source = config input, sink argument\n      = configured value) and publish USEFUL PRECISION alongside.\n  C — Narrow the definition. Config-driven paths are not TPs. One number, lower.\n\nWHAT B SETTLES:\n  - A config-driven flow REMAINS A TRUE POSITIVE on the correctness axis.\n    0001's \"exploitability is not the question\" is NOT relitigated and NOT\n    weakened. Comparability of the correctness figure with 0001/0004/0005/0006\n    is preserved by construction.\n  - A SECOND figure, \"useful precision\", is published ALONGSIDE, excluding\n    configured-action flows. This is the quantity the pre-registered band is\n    already written against (\"0006: <=25% useful => RECALL STAYS STOPPED\") and\n    which no instrument could compute -- 0006 itself calls 33.9% \"an upper bound\n    on useful precision, not a measure of it\".\n\nTHE AMENDMENT THE OWNER ACCEPTED WITH IT: the exclusion is defined\nSTRUCTURALLY, not by per-case severity or actionability judgement. Keeping\nadjudication objective was the entire point of the 0001 rubric, and a\n\"is this interesting?\" test would surrender it. A configured-action flow is\nidentified by the SHAPE of the path, not by how useful a reader would find it.\n\nNOT IN SCOPE OF THIS RULING, and must not be folded into it: vacuity that is a\nBUG rather than a rubric limit. All five shellcheck TPs are Haskell in-process\nIORef/STRef filed under untrusted-input-no-database; that is INV-nular\n(primitive kind asserted by name, never checked) and gets FIXED, not excluded.\nBoth classes produce \"true and vacuous\" findings and only one is a defect --\nconflating them would launder a bug through a rubric change.\n\nSEQUENCING THIS SITS IN (owner ruled \"fix-then-baseline, WI-gohok gates it\"):\n  WI-gohok (this, ruled) -> INV-kakad + INV-sukoh (denominator) ->\n  INV-nular / INV-fumod remaining halves -> INV-duvup (pre-register cohort +\n  claim set + rubric + estimator, then take the baseline).\nThe rubric must be fixed BEFORE the first datum: INV-duvok's own statement\nrequires it fixed in advance, so changing it mid-series restarts the series."  # 8824
